17] [17592186157482 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157482 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3237638b-b8fc-5ada-8f93-3509e05153ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157768 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157768 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157768 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157768 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $29{x}_{1}$, where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157768 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58dd07d7-85f8-5406-9b6d-4dde047be9a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157768 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-2)}{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15-4-4}{9+16+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n29{x}_{1}=29\\cdot \\frac{7}{29}=7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157769 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157769 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157769 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157769 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157769 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "824fadc8-cdf9-5224-b7eb-b17e3afd9cf8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157769 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}-\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{10}}=-\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}=-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157771 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157771 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157771 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157771 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $26{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157771 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c2da7ca-24a0-572d-977f-07408d1bbebe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157771 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 2}{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-2}{9+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n26{x}_{1}=26\\cdot \\frac{7}{13}=14.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158103 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158103 #edb/ref 1061 26151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158103 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4accdfe0-74c3-5830-95d3-ff10c16738c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158103 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158104 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158104 #edb/ref 1061 26152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158104 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "de4c2603-0fe6-5fca-ad09-e02417514da9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158104 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158105 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158105 #edb/ref 1061 26153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158105 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f247c384-fdd7-5b12-a6d5-f04393f4df78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158105 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158106 #edb/ref 1061 26173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158106 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "450eea64-01cc-591d-9042-fba21e4feb99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158106 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158470 #edb/ref 1064 "Given an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{q}_n\\}$ of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathcal{B}\\},$ there is an easy way to find the components of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in S$ with respect to this basis. The vector $\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFurthermore, if the basis is *orthonormal*, then we know that $\\mathbf{q}_i\\cdot\\mathbf{q}_i=1$. So, the formula for each component is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158470 #edb/ref 1065 4302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158470 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "67f07988-4aba-56fb-b9e7-aef39a3de89b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158470 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158471 #edb/ref 1064 "Given any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and any subspace $W$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we can always write $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of a vector from $W$ and a vector from $W^{\\perp}.$ This fact is known as the **orthogonal decomposition theorem.** We can state it formally as follows:\n\n> *Let $W$ be a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Then each $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ can be written uniquely in the form\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> where $\\mathbf{y} \\in W$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\in W^{\\perp}.$*\n\n> *Moreover, since $\\mathbf y$ is the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf x$ onto the subspace $W,$ if $\\{\\mathbf{u}_1\\,\\dots,\\mathbf{u}_p\\}$ is any orthogonal basis of $W,$ then*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{W}\\,x \\\\  & ={\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{1}}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{2}}\\,x+⋯+{\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{p}}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{1}}{{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}}{u}_{1}+\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{2}}{{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}}{u}_{2}+⋯+\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{p}}{{u}_{p}\\cdot {u}_{p}}{u}_{p}.\\end{aligned}\n> $$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158471 #edb/ref 1065 4304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158471 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "789dc38d-c1e0-5bdc-9f01-5940371a326b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158471 #edb/ref 1067 "The Orthogonal Decomposition Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235890 #edb/ref 1080 "-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186235890 #edb/ref 